  var METHODOLOGY = [
    "<strong>Scope.</strong> This index tracks listed retail prices (not transaction prices) across major U.S. online lab-grown diamond retailers. The September 2026 edition analyzed 251,098 lab-grown diamond listings across seven retailers. Data was collected on September 17, 2026 (With Clarity in three supplemental pulls the same evening, Pacific time; see Coverage this edition).",
    "<strong>Retailer panel.</strong> This edition tracks seven retailers: Ritani, Clean Origin, Grown Brilliance, With Clarity, Blue Nile, Brilliant Earth, and VRAI. The panel is fixed and disclosed for comparability. Any additions, removals, or reclassifications will be explicitly noted in that month's report.",
    "<strong>Price inputs.</strong> Prices are the publicly visible list price at the time of data collection, including any site-wide or automatic reductions that do not require a membership or promotional code. Excluded: promotional codes, membership discounts, negotiated pricing, strikethrough compare-at prices. All prices in USD, excluding sales tax and shipping.",
    "<strong>Benchmark specification.</strong> E color, VS1 clarity, round brilliant, Excellent cut, IGI-certified. Three weight cells: 1ct (0.95-1.05ct), 1.5ct (1.45-1.55ct), and 2ct (1.95-2.05ct).",
    "<strong>Statistical method.</strong> Each published figure is the median total stone price across all qualifying listings within that cell. A minimum of 30 qualifying listings is required for publication. Per-cell sample sizes (n) are visible as tooltips when hovering over any price. Total Stone Price figures in the price table (all cells except VRAI's 1.5ct, corrected in the VRAI note below) are computed as the median price-per-carat for that cell multiplied by the cell's nominal carat weight, not the raw median of actual listing prices; the two differ by up to a few percent (this edition, between 0% and 2.6% across the published IGI cells, the largest gap being Blue Nile's 1.5ct cell, $1,004 published against a raw median of $1,031). This is a candidate for a deliberate, disclosed methodology change in a future edition, not something being silently recomputed now. Clean Origin 1ct (n=20) falls below the threshold and is not published. Blue Nile 1ct (n=27) falls below the threshold and is not published. VRAI 2ct (n=11) falls below the threshold and is not published.",
    "<strong>Coverage this edition: Ritani and Grown Brilliance.</strong> Both continue to be scraped within three fixed carat bands (0.95-1.09ct, 1.40-1.59ct, 1.90-2.09ct, all colors), the collection method adopted in July. Ritani's September collection (78,635 rows) was within 5% of August's (81,986), so the overall capture is comparable; its E VS1 IGI round 1ct sample fell from 1,126 to 504 stones and 1.5ct from 1,394 to 975, a shift concentrated in this specific spec, not a smaller overall collection. Ritani's prices rose at every tracked weight this edition (see Key Findings). Grown Brilliance's 1ct sample fell from 795 to 435 stones and 2ct from 920 to 530, while its medians held flat to within 3% at every weight; its 1ct figure continues to cover 1.00-1.05ct (no listings returned between 0.95 and 0.99ct this edition either).",
    "<strong>Coverage this edition: With Clarity.</strong> With Clarity's benchmark-band collection was a full capture on all three bands this edition (1ct n=453, 1.5ct n=997, 2ct n=300), the first time since June that no band required a supplemental mobile-network pull; see the July and August editions for the capture method used when the site blocks the collecting IP. Because August's 1.5ct figure ($691/ct) was disclosed as an 85%-capture floor, part of this edition's 1.5ct increase to $711/ct may reflect the removal of that floor rather than a price move; the data available cannot separate the two effects. With Clarity is included in every Market Median computation and price range on this page.",
    "<strong>Coverage this edition: Clean Origin and Blue Nile.</strong> Clean Origin's 1.5ct cell recovered above the 30-listing minimum this edition (n=210, was n=24 in August), publishing at $263/ct; its 2ct cell held roughly flat ($279/ct, n=197, against $278/ct, n=187, in August). Its 1ct cell remains thin (n=20): still short of the minimum despite the partial recovery elsewhere. Blue Nile's E VS1 IGI round cells cleared the minimum at 1.5ct (n=56, $669/ct) and 2ct (n=54, $820/ct) for the first time since June; its 1ct cell remains thin (n=27, was n=18 in August). Because Blue Nile's own IGI series is available again for most cells, the separate Blue Nile (GIA) reference row used in July and August is retired this edition; it returns if Blue Nile's IGI cells thin again. No Blue Nile GIA row is shown this edition.",
    "<strong>Coverage this edition: what is published at 1ct.</strong> Four of the six IGI retailers have a published 1ct figure this month (Ritani, Grown Brilliance, With Clarity, Brilliant Earth), the same four as August; July's 1ct set was different (Ritani, Clean Origin, With Clarity, Brilliant Earth published; Grown Brilliance and Blue Nile thin), which is exactly why the Market Median could not test this edition's move against a July baseline and instead publishes as newly re-established (see the Market Median entry). Six IGI retailers have a published 1.5ct figure and six have a published 2ct figure this month, matching July's coverage at those cells (July also had all six); neither the 1.5ct nor the 2ct Market Median is published this edition, see below.",
    "<strong>Deduplication.</strong> Listings are deduplicated within each retailer by URL. Stones with identical IGI certificate numbers may appear at more than one retailer and are counted as independent listings at each retailer where they appear.",
    "<strong>Market Median.</strong> Computed as the median of the per-retailer median prices per carat across all IGI-panel retailers with qualifying (published) data in that cell, rounded, then multiplied by the cell's nominal weight. VRAI is excluded from this computation (non-IGI, non-comparable). Publication rule, applied from the August edition: a cell's Market Median is published only when every retailer contributing to it also had a published figure in that cell in the prior edition, and the blended month-over-month move agrees with the move of those same retailers to within five percentage points; otherwise the blended figure mostly measures who is in the panel and is withheld. 1ct passes the first test: the same four retailers (Ritani, Grown Brilliance, With Clarity, Brilliant Earth) were the only ones with a published 1ct figure in both August and September. Because August itself withheld its 1ct Market Median, there is no prior published figure to test the move against; this edition's figure, $571, is published as newly re-established rather than compared to a withheld prior value, and is the first published 1ct Market Median since June. 1.5ct and 2ct both fail the first test: Blue Nile (both cells) and Clean Origin (1.5ct only) newly cleared the publication minimum this edition and were not part of either cell's published contributor set in August, so a blended figure this month would mostly measure the panel gaining retailers, not a price move; both are withheld. The published per-retailer figures are shown individually in the price table instead.",
    "<strong>VRAI.</strong> VRAI uses Diamond Foundry proprietary grading exclusively and carries no IGI-certified inventory. Included as a non-comparable reference point. Weight windows: 1ct = 1.0-1.09ct (D-F colors); 1.5ct = 1.6-1.9ct (D-G colors); 2ct = 2.1-2.45ct (D-G colors). VRAI's 1ct (n=36) and 1.5ct (n=92) cells are published this edition; its 2ct cell (n=11) is not. The 1ct figure is median $/ct x 1 ($1,583; the raw median listing price in that window is $1,650). Because VRAI's 1.5ct window (median actual weight 1.75ct) diverges substantially from the nominal 1.5ct label, its published Total Stone Price figure ($2,145) is the true median of actual listing prices for this cell, not median $/ct ($1,250) x 1.5. VRAI's pricing was completely unchanged this cycle: of 690 lots present in both the August 29 and September 17 collections (matched by product URL), every one carries an identical price to the cent, a sharp reversal from August's +76% same-lot repricing against July. This index checked the price fields directly against the raw collections rather than assuming a currency or collection artifact.",
    "<strong>Comparability.</strong> Historical index figures may not be directly comparable when the benchmark specification or retailer panel changes. Such changes are explicitly noted in the relevant month's report. James Allen was retired from the panel in the June 2026 edition following the closure of its independent storefront. The Ritani and Grown Brilliance carat-band collection is a collection-methodology choice, not a benchmark-specification change; this edition's band request starts at 0.95ct, which brings Ritani's 1ct cell to full coverage and affects its 1ct sample as described above, while Grown Brilliance's 1ct coverage is unchanged at 1.00-1.05ct. With Clarity's 1.5ct figure is a partial capture (85%) this edition, as July's was (74%), and is a floor on the true median; see Coverage this edition.",
    "<strong>Independence.</strong> Rings.com receives no compensation from the retailers included in this index for inclusion or ranking, and no retailer has any editorial control over the methodology or results."
  ];

  function fmt(n) {
    return "$" + Math.round(n).toLocaleString("en-US") + "/ct";
  }

  function fmtDual(ppc, wt) {
    return "$" + Math.round(ppc * wt).toLocaleString("en-US");
  }

  function medianOf(arr) {
    if (!arr.length) return null;
    arr.sort(function (a, b) { return a - b; });
    var m = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
    return arr.length % 2 === 0 ? Math.round((arr[m - 1] + arr[m]) / 2) : arr[m];
  }

  function cellHtml(cell, bg, wt) {
    var base = "text-align:right;padding:10px 12px;background:" + bg + ";color:#1a1a1a;";
    if (!cell || cell.status !== "ok") {
      var tip = (cell && cell.note) || (cell && cell.status === "thin" ? "Fewer than 30 stones." : "No qualifying listings.");
      return "<td style=\"" + base + "color:#aaa;\" title=\"" + tip.replace(/"/g, "&quot;") + "\">-</td>";
    }
    var hasOverride = cell.median_total_price != null;
    var tpStr = hasOverride ? ("$" + cell.median_total_price.toLocaleString("en-US")) : fmtDual(cell.median_price_per_carat, wt || 1);
    // An override means the cell's actual weight window diverges from the
    // nominal label (VRAI 1.5ct) - flag it inline, tied to the table footnote.
    // NOTE: never put a space before "<" inside a string here - Shopify's
    // serving pipeline line-wraps before "<" after a space, which splits the
    // string literal and kills the whole script with a SyntaxError.
    var dagger = hasOverride ? "<span style=\"font-size:0.78rem;color:#888;\" title=\"Actual window diverges from the nominal weight label; see table footnote\">&nbsp;&#8224;</span>" : "";
    // partial_capture (With Clarity 1.5ct, August 2026): double dagger tied to the table footnote, note as tooltip.
    var partial = cell.partial_capture ? "<span style=\"font-size:0.78rem;color:#888;\" title=\"" + ((cell.note || "Partial capture; see the table footnote.").replace(/"/g, "&quot;")) + "\">&nbsp;&#8225;</span>" : "";
    return "<td style=\"" + base + "font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;\" title=\"n=" + cell.stone_count + "\">" + tpStr + dagger + partial + "</td>";
  }

  function nextMonthOf(monthStr) {
    var names = ["January","February","March","April","May","June","July","August","September","October","November","December"];
    var parts = (monthStr || "").split(" ");
    var i = names.indexOf(parts[0]);
    var y = parseInt(parts[1], 10) || new Date().getFullYear();
    if (i === -1) return "the next edition";
    i = (i + 1) % 12;
    if (i === 0) y++;
    return names[i] + " " + y;
  }

  var lgdHistoryData = null;
  var lgdHistoryBuiltNarrow = null;   // viewport class the current table HTML was built for
  var lgdHistoryUserTier = null;      // weight the visitor picked explicitly, if any

  function lgdHistoryNarrow() {
    return !!(window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia("(max-width: 540px)").matches);
  }

  // Weight picker above the history table. "all" is the full grid; a single
  // weight collapses the table to one column per month.
  function lgdPickerStyle(sel) {
    var base = "font-size:0.8rem;padding:4px 12px;margin:0;border-radius:3px;cursor:pointer;font-family:inherit;line-height:1.4;font-weight:600;";
    return base + (sel ? "background:#1a1a1a;color:#fff;border:1px solid #1a1a1a;" : "background:#fff;color:#1a1a1a;border:1px solid #bbb;");
  }

  function buildHistoryPicker(tier) {
    var opts = [["1ct", "1ct"], ["1.5ct", "1.5ct"], ["2ct", "2ct"], ["all", "All weights"]];
    var html = "<div id=\"lgd-history-picker\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Weight shown in the history table\" style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:6px;margin:0 0 10px;\">";
    html += "<span style=\"font-size:0.8rem;color:#555;margin-right:2px;\">Show:</span>";
    opts.forEach(function (o) {
      var sel = o[0] === tier;
      html += "<button type=\"button\" data-tier=\"" + o[0] + "\" aria-pressed=\"" + (sel ? "true" : "false") + "\" onclick=\"lgdHistoryTier(this)\" style=\"" + lgdPickerStyle(sel) + "\">" + o[1] + "</button>";
    });
    html += "</div>";
    return html;
  }

  // On phones, snap horizontal scrolling to month columns so the table never
  // rests with a column half-hidden under the pinned retailer column.
  // scroll-padding-left = the pinned column's width, measured once visible.
  // A right-hand spacer on the inner wrapper makes the maximum scroll
  // position itself a column boundary (browsers rest at the scroll end even
  // when it is not a snap position).
  function lgdHistorySnap() {
    var wrap  = document.getElementById("lgd-history-table");
    var inner = document.getElementById("lgd-history-inner");
    if (!wrap || !inner) return;
    inner.style.paddingRight = "0px";
    var row = wrap.querySelector("tbody tr");
    if (!lgdHistoryNarrow() || !row) {
      wrap.style.scrollPaddingLeft = "";
      wrap.style.scrollSnapType = "";
      return;
    }
    var cells = row.children;
    var sticky = cells[0].getBoundingClientRect();
    wrap.style.scrollPaddingLeft = Math.round(sticky.width) + "px";
    wrap.style.scrollSnapType = "x mandatory";
    var maxScroll = wrap.scrollWidth - wrap.clientWidth;
    if (0 >= maxScroll) return;
    var table = wrap.querySelector("table");
    var step = (table && parseInt(table.getAttribute("data-step"), 10)) || 1;
    for (var i = 1; cells.length > i; i += step) {
      var target = wrap.scrollLeft + cells[i].getBoundingClientRect().left - sticky.right;
      if (target > maxScroll - 0.5) {
        if (target - maxScroll > 0.5) inner.style.paddingRight = Math.ceil(target - maxScroll) + "px";
        break;
      }
    }
  }

  // The table only. tier = "all" (retailer x month x weight grid with a
  // two-row header) or one of "1ct" / "1.5ct" / "2ct" (retailer x month,
  // one column per month, fits a phone without sideways scrolling).
  function buildHistoryTable(data, tier) {
    var retailers = data.retailers || [];
    var tiers = tier === "all" ? ["1ct", "1.5ct", "2ct"] : [tier];
    // Show every month we have, oldest -> newest, so the toggle auto-extends
    // each edition. Cap the columns to the most recent 12 months so the table
    // stays usable; the JSON retains the full record regardless.
    var months = data.history.slice(-12);
    var narrow = lgdHistoryNarrow();
    lgdHistoryBuiltNarrow = narrow;
    var pad  = narrow ? "7px 4px" : "9px 10px";
    var hpad = narrow ? "4px" : "10px";
    var name = narrow ? "white-space:normal;min-width:76px;" : "white-space:nowrap;";
    // max-width on a table cell is ignored by auto table layout; cap the
    // name via an inner block instead.
    var nameOpen  = narrow ? "<div style=\"max-width:84px;\">" : "";
    var nameClose = narrow ? "</div>" : "";
    var snap = narrow ? "scroll-snap-align:start;" : "";
    // The retailer column stays pinned while the months scroll. box-shadow,
    // not border-right: borders on a collapsed table do not travel with a
    // sticky cell.
    var stick = "position:sticky;left:0;z-index:2;box-shadow:2px 0 0 #e0e0e0;";

    var thL = "text-align:left;padding:" + pad + ";border-bottom:2px solid #1a1a1a;font-weight:700;color:#1a1a1a;background:#fff;vertical-align:bottom;" + name + stick;
    var thG = "text-align:center;padding:6px " + hpad + " 5px;font-weight:700;font-size:0.8rem;color:#1a1a1a;background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;white-space:nowrap;";
    var thR = "text-align:right;padding:6px " + hpad + ";border-bottom:2px solid #1a1a1a;font-weight:700;font-size:0.8rem;white-space:nowrap;color:#1a1a1a;background:#fff;vertical-align:bottom;";

    var html = "<table data-tier=\"" + tier + "\" data-step=\"" + tiers.length + "\" style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:" + (narrow ? "0.78rem" : "0.85rem") + ";\">";
    if (tiers.length > 1) {
      // Header: month groups on top, weight tiers beneath.
      html += "<thead><tr><th rowspan=\"2\" style=\"" + thL + "\">Retailer</th>";
      months.forEach(function (m, idx) {
        var sep = idx > 0 ? "border-left:1px solid #e0e0e0;" : "";
        html += "<th colspan=\"3\" style=\"" + thG + sep + "\">" + m.month + "</th>";
      });
      html += "</tr><tr>";
      months.forEach(function (m, idx) {
        tiers.forEach(function (t, ti) {
          var sep = (idx > 0 && ti === 0) ? "border-left:1px solid #e0e0e0;" : "";
          html += "<th style=\"" + thR + sep + (ti === 0 ? snap : "") + "\">" + t + "</th>";
